---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/variantPlayground.css';
import { SPEC_NOTES } from '../lib/shorkieModel';

// A `bare` page, so it carries its own header and needs its own <h1> -- audit:indexing asserts a
// visible heading on every sitemap URL. The page is NOT at /shorkie/: that path already serves the
// project documentation site on this domain.
const title = 'The Live Variant Playground — Shorkie, layer by layer';
const description =
  'An in-browser sandbox running the real Shorkie fungal sequence-to-function model: edit DNA and watch the conv-stem neurons fire live, then run the full 14 M-parameter network to predict RNA-seq coverage across a 16,384 bp yeast window.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} bare={true}>
  <div class="vp" data-vp>
    <header class="vp-top">
      <h1 class="vp-title">The Live Variant Playground</h1>
      <span class="vp-sub">Shorkie · Chao et al. 2025 · fold f0, 14,253,567 parameters</span>
      <span class="vp-spacer"></span>
      <a class="vp-sub" href="/posts/shorkie/">Read the post →</a>
      <a class="vp-sub" href="/">Home</a>
    </header>

    <div class="vp-scroll">
    <div class="vp-body">
        <section class="vp-panel">
          <h2>Sequence</h2>
          <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-bottom:0.55rem;">
            <select class="vp-select" data-vp-locus aria-label="Preset locus"></select>
            <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-livestat></span>
          </div>
          <div class="vp-motifs" data-vp-motifs hidden>
            <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0 0 0.35rem;">
              <strong>Figure 4 motifs.</strong> Database consensus matches found in this window on
              either strand — not the paper's own annotations, which this scan neither reproduces
              exactly nor claims to. Click one to scramble it and re-run: same length, same base
              composition, only the order destroyed, so any change is attributable to the arrangement
              rather than to GC content. The effect is measured over <em>this window's own gene</em>,
              not the whole window — a 14,336 bp yeast window holds a dozen genes and the tallest is
              usually not the one whose promoter you edited.
            </p>
            <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0 0 0.35rem;">
              Expect a wide range. Across these six windows the splicing motifs dominate — knocking
              out DTD1's 5′ splice site costs <strong>34%</strong> of its predicted expression and its
              branch point 21% — while transcription-factor sites range from −18.3% (HOP2's TATA box) through
              −10.5% (KRE33's Reb1) and −7.4% (FUN12's RRPE) down to sites that move nothing at all. A near-zero result is a real
              answer about that site, not a broken button.
            </p>
            <div class="vp-motif-row" data-vp-motif-list></div>
            <p class="vp-stat" data-vp-knockout></p>
          </div>
          <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
            Each locus is a <strong>16,384 bp window</strong> — the model's full context — centred on the
            named gene. Six of them are the windows Figure 4 of the paper prints, at the published
            coordinates; the rest are classic high-expressers and the galactose regulon.
          </p>
        </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------- full-width flow -->
      <section class="vp-panel vp-flow-panel">
        <h2>The network, end to end — watch a sequence pass through it</h2>
        <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;">
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-view="2d" aria-pressed="true">flat</button>
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-view="3d" aria-pressed="false">volume</button>
          <span class="vp-stat" style="margin-left:0.5rem">showing</span>
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-showing="activation" aria-pressed="true">activation</button>
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-showing="relevance" aria-pressed="false">relevance for the traced region</button>
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-spin hidden>↻ resume rotation</button>
          <span class="vp-stat">Same layout either way: height is positions, width is channels.</span>
        </div>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas vp-flow3d" data-vp-flow3d hidden role="img"
                aria-label="The network as slabs along a depth axis; drag to orbit"></canvas>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas vp-flow" data-vp-flow role="img"
                aria-label="Shorkie architecture: 20 stages from the 16,384 bp input to 896 output bins"></canvas>
        <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-play aria-pressed="false">▶ Play</button>
          <input class="vp-range" type="range" min="0" max="1000" value="1000" data-vp-scrub
                 style="flex:1;min-width:9rem" aria-label="Position of the wavefront through the network" />
          <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-stagestat></span>
        </div>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;">
          Each block is one stage, sized by its real dimensions on the log scales named above. Click a stage to inspect it.
          Before you run the model the blocks are empty outlines; afterwards they fill with the
          activations from that same forward pass.
          <br /><br />
          <strong>Switch to “relevance” and the same blocks show one region instead of the whole
          window.</strong> Trace a region below, and every stage repaints with what it contributed to
          <em>that region alone</em> — every other output bin masked out. Both of the map's margins
          are exact: the per-channel one and the per-position one are each a row-sum of precomputed
          groups, because gradients are linear in which outputs you select. Its interior is their
          outer product, which is the only reconstruction consistent with both margins if channel and
          position act independently — a real assumption, and the reason the panel names it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------- layer detail -->
      <section class="vp-panel" data-vp-stage-detail>
        <h2>This layer, in detail <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-stage-title></span>
          <canvas class="vp-aspect" data-vp-aspect width="34" height="22" role="img"
                  aria-label="The tensor's real proportions: width is positions, height is channels"
          ></canvas></h2>
        <div class="vp-controls">
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-tab="activation" aria-pressed="true">activation</button>
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-tab="attention" aria-pressed="false" hidden>attention</button>
          <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-stage-top></span>
          <span class="vp-legend" data-vp-legend aria-hidden="true"></span>
        </div>
        <ol class="vp-sublayers" data-vp-sublayers></ol>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas" data-vp-stage-map role="img"
                aria-label="Activation map for the selected layer"></canvas>
        <p class="vp-notes" data-vp-stage-note style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;"></p>
      </section>


        <section class="vp-panel">
          <h2>Predicted coverage — the full model</h2>
          <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
            <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-run>Load model — live layers &amp; editing</button>
            <span class="vp-status" data-vp-status></span>
          </div>
          <svg class="vp-svg" data-vp-track role="img"
               aria-label="Predicted RNA-seq coverage across 896 bins"></svg>
          <canvas class="vp-canvas vp-attr" data-vp-attr role="img"
                  aria-label="Which input bases drive the selected output region"></canvas>
          <canvas class="vp-canvas" data-vp-methods role="img"
                  aria-label="Every interpretability method on one axis, aligned with the annotation"></canvas>
          <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
            <strong>Four methods, one axis, one annotation.</strong> They do not measure the same
            thing and are not expected to agree everywhere:
            <em>mutagenesis</em> substitutes one base and re-runs the model;
            <em>gradient × input</em> is a local linear sensitivity at the reference base, mean-centred
            across the four bases as Borzoi does;
            <em>integrated gradients</em> accumulates that gradient along a path from an all-zero
            baseline and is the only one here whose values <em>sum</em> to the prediction difference —
            a property it would lose if it were mean-centred, so it is not;
            <em>occlusion</em> ablates a whole 64 bp stretch and measures what the model loses.
            Where they agree the result is robust; where they diverge, the divergence is the finding.
            A method that covers only part of the window is drawn in place with the rest left blank,
            which is what the paper does for its own partial windows.
          </p>
          <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-top:0.35rem;">
            <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-trace-label>Drag across the curve above to trace a region back to the sequence.</span>
            <select class="vp-select" data-vp-anchor aria-label="Trace a gene at single-base resolution"></select>
            <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-trace-clear>clear</button>
          </div>
          <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
            <strong>Traceback.</strong> Gradient × input: how much the prediction over the selected
            bins moves per unit of each input base, times the base that is actually there. It is a{' '}
            <em>local sensitivity, not a decomposition</em> — the values do not sum to the
            prediction. Dragging is exact rather than interpolated, because gradients superpose:
            the attribution for a set of bins is the sum of their individual gradients. Dragging
            resolves to 128 bp; picking a gene gives single-base resolution.
          </p>
          <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
            <label class="vp-stat" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:0.35rem;">
              <input type="checkbox" data-vp-truth checked /> measured coverage
            </label>
            <select class="vp-select" data-vp-group aria-label="Output track group"></select>
            <label class="vp-stat" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:0.35rem;">
              <input type="checkbox" data-vp-logaxis checked /> log axis
            </label>
            <label class="vp-stat" style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:0.35rem;">
              <input type="checkbox" data-vp-generows checked /> one row per gene
            </label>
            <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-truthstat></span>
          </div>
          <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;">
            <strong>The curve is already here.</strong> Every preset locus was run offline at the full
            16,384 bp context and the predictions ship with the page, so there is nothing to wait for.
            Loading the model — a 28.6 MB download and about 17 s of WebAssembly per run — is what
            gives you the live layer activations, sequence editing and motif knockouts.
            896 bins of 16 bp across the window's 14,336 bp interior. Purple bars are annotated ORFs.
            A dashed line, once you mutate, is the unedited reference. <strong>Measured coverage</strong>{' '}
            is the second curve where it is loaded: Shorkie's own BigWigs binned exactly as its training
            labels were — 16 bp sums, soft-clipped, 1,024 bp cropped from each end — so the two curves are
            the same quantity and Pearson <em>r</em> means something. The bucket they live in is
            requester-pays, so this site ships none of them; <code>scripts/shorkie/make_truth.py</code>
            builds the overlay from a local copy.
          </p>
        </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------- interpretation -->
      <section class="vp-panel">
        <h2>Trace a region — layer by layer, back to the sequence</h2>
        <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-region-prev>‹ prev</button>
          <select class="vp-select" data-vp-region aria-label="Region to trace"></select>
          <button type="button" class="vp-btn" data-vp-region-next>next ›</button>
          <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-region-stat></span>
        </div>
        <div class="vp-trace-grid">
          <ol class="vp-stages" data-vp-stage-profile></ol>
          <div>
            <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0 0 0.4rem;">
              <strong>Every stage, for one region.</strong> Each bar is how much of the selected
              region's relevance passes through that stage — a <em>mean</em> over the stage's
              channels, never a sum, because summing would rank a 1,536-channel stage above a
              384-channel one on width alone, which is a fact about the architecture and not about
              this region. Click a stage to open it above; click a channel to plot that neuron.
            </p>
            <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0 0 0.4rem;">
              <strong>Attention rollout</strong> is a second, independent answer. It composes the
              eight attention matrices — each mixed half-and-half with the identity to account for
              the residual stream — so it reports what the transformer <em>can</em> read for this
              region. That is an architectural quantity, not an attribution: where it disagrees with
              gradient × input, the disagreement is the interesting part, not a bug.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas" data-vp-stage-stack role="img"
                aria-label="Where each stage's contributing neurons fire across the window, one row per stage"
                style="margin-top:0.5rem"></canvas>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          <strong>One row per stage, the window along x.</strong> Each row is that stage's activation
          weighted by how much each channel carried the selected region — <em>where the neurons that
          matter for this region actually fire</em>. Each row is painted against its
          <em>own mean</em> — red where that stage draws more than average, blue less, neutral where it
          has no positional preference. The early residual blocks come out close to neutral, and that
          is a real property: pooled to 128 positions, a convolution over 16,384 is spatially almost
          uniform. Structure appears from the transformer on, which is where the receptive field is
          the whole window and the model can choose where to look.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          It is the <em>factorised</em> combination — per-channel relevance times per-position
          activation — not a per-position gradient, because the shipped pack carries relevance summed
          over position. It answers “where do the channels that matter fire”, which is not quite the
          same question as “which positions the gradient flows through”.
        </p>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas" data-vp-rollout role="img"
                aria-label="Attention rollout: which parts of the window the transformer reads for this region"
                style="margin-top:0.5rem"></canvas>
      </section>

      <section class="vp-panel">
        <h2>The bases themselves <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-logo-stat></span></h2>
        <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;">
          <input class="vp-range" type="range" min="0" max="1000" value="500" data-vp-logo-pan
                 style="flex:1;min-width:9rem" aria-label="Position of the sequence logo window" />
          <select class="vp-select" data-vp-logo-source aria-label="Attribution source">
            <option value="ism" selected>mutagenesis (the paper's)</option>
            <option value="grad">gradient × input</option>
          </select>
          <select class="vp-select" data-vp-logo-width aria-label="Logo window width">
            <option value="60">60 bp</option>
            <option value="150" selected>150 bp</option>
            <option value="400">400 bp</option>
          </select>
        </div>
        <svg class="vp-svg" data-vp-seq-logo role="img"
             aria-label="Gradient times input attribution as a sequence logo"></svg>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;">
          <strong>Drawn exactly as the paper draws it</strong> — the real DejaVu Sans Bold glyphs at
          the paper's own per-letter offsets, scaled 1.35× on both axes, A green / C blue / G orange /
          T red, stacked largest-magnitude nearest the axis with positives up and negatives mirrored
          below. Letters overflow their column and touch; that density is the published look, not a
          bug. The colours are fixed in every theme, because a figure's base colours are part of the
          figure.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          <strong>Two sources, and they are not the same method.</strong> <em>Mutagenesis</em> is the
          paper's Figure 4 quantity: every substitution actually run, then mean-centred across the
          four bases and projected on the reference, so the height reads as “how much the base that
          is <em>there</em> matters” rather than “what this one substitution did”. It covers the
          promoter window only, so panning outside it falls back to the gradient and says so. <em>Gradient × input</em> is a local linear
          sensitivity — it is identically zero at the three bases not present, because the input is
          one-hot, so it draws one letter per position. <strong>The paper's published figures use no
          gradients at all</strong>; its single gradient routine is unreached scaffolding that still
          declares a 4-channel input the 170-channel model cannot accept. Gradient × input is here
          because it is instant, not because it is what the paper did.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="vp-panel">
        <h2>What drives what <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-occl-stat></span></h2>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas vp-occl" data-vp-occl role="img"
                aria-label="Occlusion map: input window on x, output bin on y, colour is what that output loses"></canvas>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;">
          <strong>Every input window against every output bin.</strong> Each column is a 64 bp stretch
          of the input, ablated; each row is one of the 896 output bins; the colour is what that bin
          loses when that stretch is gone. Blue is a loss, red a gain. This is the only genuinely
          two-dimensional view on the page — every other method collapses to a single profile over
          the input — and it is measured, not inferred: 256 real forward passes a locus.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          <strong>Read it as a map, and read both parts.</strong> The dark diagonal is local effect —
          a window damaging the output directly above it — and per cell it is by far the strongest
          thing here. But it is also narrow: one window of 256. Summed across the row, the local
          footprint accounts for only <strong>0.3–8%</strong> of the most damaging window's total
          effect, so the diagonal is where the model is <em>most intense</em> and the off-diagonal is
          where most of what it does actually lives. Neither statement alone is the truth.
          Off the diagonal, a <em>vertical stripe</em> is an input stretch many outputs depend on and
          a <em>horizontal stripe</em> is an output bin that reads widely.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          Ablation zeroes the four DNA channels, which is exactly how the paper's language model masks
          a position and is indistinguishable from a run of N. That is a different question from the
          motif knockouts above, which <em>shuffle</em>: a shuffle keeps base composition and asks
          whether the arrangement matters, while zeroing asks whether the stretch carries information
          at all.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="vp-panel">
        <h2>In-silico mutagenesis <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-ism-stat></span></h2>
        <svg class="vp-svg" data-vp-ism-logo role="img"
             aria-label="Mutagenesis saliency as the paper's sequence logo, with motif and splice annotations"></svg>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0.5rem;">
          <strong>This is the paper's Figure 4 panel, reproduced.</strong> Mean-centred across the
          four bases and projected on the reference, so each position carries one letter — the
          importance of the base that is actually <em>there</em>, rather than what one substitution
          did. Red dashed boxes are matched motifs and splice landmarks; click one to zoom the
          sequence logo above onto it. The raster below keeps the full position × base view, which
          the paper computes but never plots.
        </p>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas vp-ism" data-vp-ism role="img"
                aria-label="Every substitution in the promoter window and its measured effect"></canvas>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;">
          <strong>Every base mutated to all three alternatives, and the model re-run.</strong> Not a
          gradient and not an approximation: each cell is a real forward pass, so it is the model's
          own answer to “what if this base were different”, with no linearity assumption. The plane is
          <em>mutant minus reference</em>, so it takes the same colours as every other raster here:
          <strong>blue is negative — the substitution lowers the prediction — and red raises it.</strong>
          The reference base's own cell is zero by definition, which is why one row of every column
          is blank.
          The measured quantity is the RNA-seq group mean over <em>this window's own gene</em> — the
          same quantity the motif knockouts report, because a 14,336 bp window holds a dozen genes
          and the tallest is rarely the one whose promoter you edited.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          <strong>Scope.</strong> A full window is 49,152 substitutions and about seventy minutes a
          locus, so this is 512 bp centred on the gene's transcription start — the core promoter.
          It is the honest local answer for that promoter and says nothing about the rest of the
          window.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          <strong>The unit is logSED</strong> — <code>log2(Σ_alt + 1) − log2(Σ_ref + 1)</code> summed
          over the gene's own body bins and averaged over the <strong>384 T0 RNA-seq tracks</strong>,
          which is the paper's own variant score. Being a log ratio, it is directly comparable across
          genes: −1 is a halving whether the promoter is silent or maximal. An earlier version of this
          panel reported a linear difference as a percentage, which made HOP2's +448% look like the
          largest effect on the page when it was a move of 1.9 on a reference of 0.42. A log ratio is
          how the paper avoided that, and the panel now uses it.
        </p>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.35rem 0 0;">
          <strong>An independent check.</strong> DTD1's strongest single substitution lands on bp
          8,165 — the exact <code>GT</code> donor of its 71 bp intron — at −38.5%. The motif panel
          above reaches the same site by an unrelated route, scrambling the whole 5′ splice site for
          −34%. Two methods that share no arithmetic agreeing on one base is the strongest evidence
          on this page that the pipeline is right.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------- every output track -->
      <section class="vp-panel">
        <h2>Every output track <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-heat-stat></span></h2>
        <canvas class="vp-canvas vp-heat" data-vp-heat role="img"
                aria-label="All 5,215 predicted output tracks, one row each, grouped by assay"></canvas>
        <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-top:0.5rem;">
          <select class="vp-select" data-vp-pick-group aria-label="Assay block"></select>
          <select class="vp-select" data-vp-pick-key aria-label="Regulator, ChIP target or run"></select>
          <select class="vp-select" data-vp-pick-track aria-label="Timepoint or replicate"></select>
        </div>
        <div class="vp-controls" style="margin-top:0.35rem;">
          <span class="vp-stat" data-vp-track-name></span>
        </div>
        <svg class="vp-svg" data-vp-single role="img"
             aria-label="The selected output track across the window"></svg>
        <p class="vp-notes" style="margin:0.4rem 0 0;">
          One row per predicted experiment, in the order the released targets sheet lists them, so
          the four assay blocks read as bands. Click a row to plot that single track below — the
          overview above averages thousands of experiments, which is a curve no instrument would
          produce. Where rows outnumber pixels each drawn row is the maximum over the tracks it covers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------- reference column -->
      <div>
        <section class="vp-panel">
          <h2>The network, end to end</h2>
          <ol class="vp-layers" data-vp-layers></ol>
        </section>

        <section class="vp-panel">
          <h2>Paper vs checkpoint</h2>
          <div class="vp-notes">
            <p style="margin:0 0 0.4rem;">
              Seven places where the published Methods and the released f0 checkpoint disagree. This page
              follows the checkpoint, because that is the model actually running.
            </p>
            <table>
              <tbody>
                {SPEC_NOTES.map((n) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{n.topic}</th>
                    <td><em>paper:</em> {n.paper}<br /><em>checkpoint:</em> {n.checkpoint}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
      </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../scripts/variantPlayground';
</script>
